IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Criminal Code Act 1995 (Commonwealth of Australia), Schedule (the Criminal Code) -- what the accused person meets: whether
-- Chapter 2 governs the offence (s 2.2); the elements of an offence and what must be proved (ss 3.1, 3.2); voluntariness
-- (s 4.2); the fault elements and the defaults (ss 5.1-5.6); strict and absolute liability (ss 6.1, 6.2); the defences in Part
-- 2.3 -- age (ss 7.1, 7.2), mental impairment (s 7.3), intoxication (Division 8), mistake of fact and of law and claim of right
-- (Division 9), intervening conduct, duress, sudden or extraordinary emergency, self-defence and lawful authority (Division
-- 10); the burden and standard of proof (Part 2.6); and two offences a member of the public meets: general dishonesty against
-- a Commonwealth entity (s 135.1(1), (3), (5), with s 130.3) and using a carriage service to menace, harass or cause offence
-- (s 474.17, with s 473.4).
--
-- SEAT: the accused person -- "What does the prosecution have to prove? I didn't mean to -- does that matter? I was drunk. I
-- thought it was mine. I was threatened. I was defending myself. I was 13. Is what I posted an offence?" Provisions addressed to
-- the court or the tribunal of fact are encoded as what they mean for me. Every question the Code leaves to the tribunal of
-- fact (whether a risk was unjustifiable, whether reasonable persons would regard something as offensive, whether conduct was
-- dishonest by ordinary standards) is asked as an input, never guessed.
--
-- Edition: the compilation registered as C2026C00243 (Compilation No. 174, compilation date 30 June 2026; Federal Register of
-- Legislation, retrieved 13 October 2026), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A04868.txt.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. Chapter 1 beyond s 1.1; s 4.3 (omissions) beyond voluntariness; Part 2.4 (attempt, complicity, incitement,
-- conspiracy); Part 2.5 (corporate criminal responsibility); Part 2.7 (geographical jurisdiction); Chapters 4 to 10 except
-- ss 130.3, 135.1(1), (3), (5), 473.4, 474.17. Conduct before 15 December 2001 for an offence outside the Code answers
-- `not covered` (s 2.2(2)).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the accused's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Criminal Code -- the accused person`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    -- s 2.2(2)
    `my offence is not in the Code and my conduct was before 15 December 2001: Chapter 2 does not apply to it (s 2.2(2)), and the common law principles that then applied are not encoded`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

§§ `s 2.2 -- do these general principles govern my offence`

-- s 2.2: "(1) This Chapter applies to all offences against this Code. (2) Subject to section 2.3, this Chapter applies on and
-- after 15 December 2001 to all other offences." s 2.3: the intoxication provisions (ss 4.2(6), (7), Division 8) "apply to all
-- offences". s 2.1: Chapter 2 "contains all the general principles of criminal responsibility that apply to any offence,
-- irrespective of how the offence is created." The applicable day is read as the day of my conduct.
DECLARE `Does Chapter 2 govern my offence` IS ONE OF
    `yes: Chapter 2 applies to every offence against the Code (s 2.2(1))`
    `yes: Chapter 2 applies to this Commonwealth offence outside the Code, because my conduct was on or after 15 December 2001 (s 2.2(2))`

@export Do the Criminal Code's general principles of criminal responsibility apply to the Commonwealth offence I am charged with?
GIVEN `the offence is in the Criminal Code` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence you are charged with is created by the Criminal Code itself (rather than by another Commonwealth Act, such as the Crimes Act 1914 or the Taxation Administration Act 1953)
      `the day of my conduct` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you did the conduct the charge is about
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Does Chapter 2 govern my offence`
`Does Chapter 2 govern my offence question` MEANS
    IF `the offence is in the Criminal Code`
    THEN `answered` `yes: Chapter 2 applies to every offence against the Code (s 2.2(1))`
    ELSE IF `the day of my conduct` AT LEAST (Date 15 12 2001)
         THEN `answered` `yes: Chapter 2 applies to this Commonwealth offence outside the Code, because my conduct was on or after 15 December 2001 (s 2.2(2))`
         ELSE `not covered` `my offence is not in the Code and my conduct was before 15 December 2001: Chapter 2 does not apply to it (s 2.2(2)), and the common law principles that then applied are not encoded`

§§ `ss 3.1-6.2 -- what the prosecution must prove`

-- s 3.1(1): "An offence consists of physical elements and fault elements." (2): the law creating the offence "may provide that
-- there is no fault element for one or more physical elements". s 3.2: to be found guilty, the prosecution must prove "(a) the
-- existence of such physical elements as are ... relevant to establishing guilt; (b) in respect of each such physical element
-- for which a fault element is required, one of the fault elements for the physical element." s 4.1(1): a physical element "may
-- be: (a) conduct; or (b) a result of conduct; or (c) a circumstance in which conduct, or a result of conduct, occurs."
-- s 5.6: "(1) If the law creating the offence does not specify a fault element for a physical element that consists only of
-- conduct, intention is the fault element ... (2) ... for a physical element that consists of a circumstance or a result,
-- recklessness is the fault element". s 5.4(4): where recklessness is the fault element, "proof of intention, knowledge or
-- recklessness will satisfy" it. s 6.1(1): strict liability -- "no fault elements ... and ... the defence of mistake of fact
-- under section 9.2 is available"; s 6.2(1): absolute liability -- no fault elements and s 9.2 "is unavailable".
DECLARE `Kind of physical element` IS ONE OF
    `conduct`
    `a circumstance`
    `a result of conduct`

DECLARE `Fault element` IS ONE OF
    `intention`
    `knowledge`
    `recklessness`
    `negligence`
    `none stated`

DECLARE `What fault must be proved for this element` IS ONE OF
    `intention: the offence states no fault element for this conduct element, so intention is the fault element (s 5.6(1))`
    `recklessness: the offence states no fault element for this circumstance or result, so recklessness is the fault element, and proof of intention or knowledge also satisfies it (ss 5.6(2), 5.4(4))`
    `the fault element the offence states for it (ss 3.1(3), 5.1)`
    `none: strict liability applies to this element, so no fault need be proved, but my reasonable mistake of fact under s 9.2 is a defence (s 6.1)`
    `none: absolute liability applies to this element, so no fault need be proved, and a mistake of fact under s 9.2 is no defence (s 6.2)`

GIVEN `the kind of element` IS A `Kind of physical element`
      `the fault element the offence states` IS A `Fault element`
      `strict liability applies to it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `absolute liability applies to it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What fault must be proved for this element`
`the fault required` MEANS
    IF `absolute liability applies to it`
    THEN `none: absolute liability applies to this element, so no fault need be proved, and a mistake of fact under s 9.2 is no defence (s 6.2)`
    ELSE IF `strict liability applies to it`
         THEN `none: strict liability applies to this element, so no fault need be proved, but my reasonable mistake of fact under s 9.2 is a defence (s 6.1)`
         ELSE IF (NOT (`the fault element the offence states` EQUALS `none stated`))
              THEN `the fault element the offence states for it (ss 3.1(3), 5.1)`
              ELSE IF `the kind of element` EQUALS `conduct`
                   THEN `intention: the offence states no fault element for this conduct element, so intention is the fault element (s 5.6(1))`
                   ELSE `recklessness: the offence states no fault element for this circumstance or result, so recklessness is the fault element, and proof of intention or knowledge also satisfies it (ss 5.6(2), 5.4(4))`

@export For one element of the offence I am charged with, what state of mind must the prosecution prove I had?
GIVEN `the kind of element` IS A `Kind of physical element` @desc Whether this element of the offence is your conduct (what you did or failed to do), a circumstance in which it happened, or a result of it
      `the fault element the offence states` IS A `Fault element` @desc The fault element the law creating the offence states for this element (intention, knowledge, recklessness or negligence), or "none stated" if it says none
      `strict liability applies to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law creating the offence says the offence, or this element, is one of strict liability
      `absolute liability applies to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law creating the offence says the offence, or this element, is one of absolute liability
GIVETH A `What fault must be proved for this element`
`What fault must be proved for this element question` MEANS
    `the fault required` `the kind of element` `the fault element the offence states` `strict liability applies to it` `absolute liability applies to it`

-- s 5.2: "(1) A person has intention with respect to conduct if he or she means to engage in that conduct. (2) ... with respect
-- to a circumstance if he or she believes that it exists or will exist. (3) ... with respect to a result if he or she means to
-- bring it about or is aware that it will occur in the ordinary course of events." s 5.3: "knowledge of a circumstance or a
-- result if he or she is aware that it exists or will exist in the ordinary course of events." s 5.4(1), (2): reckless as to a
-- circumstance or result if "aware of a substantial risk" and, "having regard to the circumstances known to him or her, it is
-- unjustifiable to take the risk"; (3) "whether taking a risk is unjustifiable is one of fact"; (4) intention or knowledge also
-- satisfy recklessness. s 5.5: negligent if the conduct "involves: (a) such a great falling short of the standard of care that a
-- reasonable person would exercise ...; and (b) such a high risk that the physical element exists or will exist; that the
-- conduct merits criminal punishment" (asked as the tribunal of fact's finding). Knowledge and recklessness are defined only
-- for circumstances and results. The deposited text has no rule that intention, knowledge or recklessness satisfies negligence
-- (NOTES.md finding 4); this encoding does not add one.
GIVEN `the kind of element` IS A `Kind of physical element`
      `the fault element required` IS A `Fault element`
      `I meant it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I believed it existed or would exist` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I was aware it would happen or exist in the ordinary course` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I was aware of a substantial risk of it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court finds taking the risk was unjustifiable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court finds my conduct criminally negligent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `my state of mind meets the fault element` IF
    CONSIDER `the fault element required`
    WHEN `intention`    THEN `I had intention`
    WHEN `knowledge`    THEN `I had knowledge`
    WHEN `recklessness` THEN `I had intention` OR `I had knowledge` OR `I was reckless`
    WHEN `negligence`   THEN `the court finds my conduct criminally negligent`
    OTHERWISE TRUE
    WHERE
        `I had intention` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the kind of element`
            WHEN `conduct`        THEN `I meant it`
            WHEN `a circumstance` THEN `I believed it existed or would exist`
            OTHERWISE `I meant it` OR `I was aware it would happen or exist in the ordinary course`
        `I had knowledge` MEANS
                (NOT (`the kind of element` EQUALS `conduct`))
            AND `I was aware it would happen or exist in the ordinary course`
        `I was reckless` MEANS
                (NOT (`the kind of element` EQUALS `conduct`))
            AND `I was aware of a substantial risk of it`
            AND `the court finds taking the risk was unjustifiable`

@export Did I have the state of mind (intention, knowledge, recklessness or negligence) the offence requires for this element?
GIVEN `the kind of element` IS A `Kind of physical element` @desc Whether this element is your conduct, a circumstance, or a result of your conduct
      `the fault element required` IS A `Fault element` @desc The fault element required for this element (from the offence, or the s 5.6 default: intention for conduct, recklessness for a circumstance or result); "none stated" if strict or absolute liability applies
      `I meant it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For conduct: whether you meant to do it. For a result: whether you meant to bring it about
      `I believed it existed or would exist` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a circumstance: whether you believed it existed or would exist
      `I was aware it would happen or exist in the ordinary course` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a circumstance or result: whether you were aware that it existed, or would exist or happen in the ordinary course of events
      `I was aware of a substantial risk of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a circumstance or result: whether you were aware of a substantial risk that it existed or would exist or happen
      `the court finds taking the risk was unjustifiable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court (or jury) finds that, given the circumstances known to you, it was unjustifiable to take that risk
      `the court finds my conduct criminally negligent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court (or jury) finds your conduct fell so far short of the care a reasonable person would take, with so high a risk, that it merits criminal punishment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Did I have the fault element for this element` IF
    `my state of mind meets the fault element` `the kind of element` `the fault element required` `I meant it` `I believed it existed or would exist` `I was aware it would happen or exist in the ordinary course` `I was aware of a substantial risk of it` `the court finds taking the risk was unjustifiable` `the court finds my conduct criminally negligent`

-- s 4.2: "(1) Conduct can only be a physical element if it is voluntary. (2) Conduct is only voluntary if it is a product of the
-- will of the person ..." (3): not voluntary -- "a spasm, convulsion or other unwilled bodily movement", an act "during sleep
-- or unconsciousness", or during "impaired consciousness depriving the person of the will to act". (4): an omission is
-- voluntary only if I was "capable of performing" the act. (5): a state of affairs only if I was "capable of exercising
-- control". (6): "Evidence of self-induced intoxication cannot be considered in determining whether conduct is voluntary."
-- s 7.3(6): mental impairment cannot be relied on "to deny voluntariness" (only to deny responsibility under s 7.3).
DECLARE `Was my conduct voluntary` IS ONE OF
    `no: my conduct was not a product of my will (for example a spasm, or an act in sleep or unconsciousness), or I could not have done the act omitted or controlled the state of affairs, so it cannot be a physical element of the offence (s 4.2)`
    `yes, for this purpose: what made it unwilled was self-induced intoxication, which cannot be considered on voluntariness (s 4.2(6))`
    `yes, for this purpose: what made it unwilled was a mental impairment, which cannot be used to deny voluntariness; consider s 7.3 instead (s 7.3(6))`
    `yes: my conduct was a product of my will`

@export I did not act of my own will (for example I was asleep, or had a seizure). Can my conduct count against me?
GIVEN `my conduct was a product of my will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what you did (or the omission or state of affairs) was a product of your will; FALSE for a spasm, convulsion or unwilled movement, an act in sleep or unconsciousness or impaired consciousness, an omission to do something you could not do, or a state of affairs you could not control
      `the cause was self-induced intoxication` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the only reason it was not willed was intoxication that was self-induced (not involuntary, and not caused by fraud, emergency, accident, reasonable mistake, duress or force)
      `the cause was a mental impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the only reason it was not willed was a mental impairment (such as a mental illness or intellectual disability)
GIVETH A `Was my conduct voluntary`
`Was my conduct voluntary question` MEANS
    IF `my conduct was a product of my will`
    THEN `yes: my conduct was a product of my will`
    ELSE IF `the cause was self-induced intoxication`
         THEN `yes, for this purpose: what made it unwilled was self-induced intoxication, which cannot be considered on voluntariness (s 4.2(6))`
         ELSE IF `the cause was a mental impairment`
              THEN `yes, for this purpose: what made it unwilled was a mental impairment, which cannot be used to deny voluntariness; consider s 7.3 instead (s 7.3(6))`
              ELSE `no: my conduct was not a product of my will (for example a spasm, or an act in sleep or unconsciousness), or I could not have done the act omitted or controlled the state of affairs, so it cannot be a physical element of the offence (s 4.2)`

§§ `ss 7.1, 7.2, 7.3 -- if you were a child, or had a mental impairment, at the time`

-- s 7.1: "A child under 10 years old is not criminally responsible for an offence." s 7.2(1): "A child aged 10 years or more but
-- under 14 years old can only be criminally responsible for an offence if the child knows that his or her conduct is wrong."
-- (2): "The question whether a child knows that his or her conduct is wrong is one of fact. The burden of proving this is on the
-- prosecution."
DECLARE `Can I be responsible at my age` IS ONE OF
    `no: I was under 10, and a child under 10 is not criminally responsible for an offence (s 7.1)`
    `only if the prosecution proves I knew my conduct was wrong: I was 10 or more but under 14 (s 7.2)`
    `no: I was 10 or more but under 14, and it is not proved that I knew my conduct was wrong (s 7.2)`
    `yes: I was 10 or more but under 14, and the prosecution has proved I knew my conduct was wrong (s 7.2)`
    `yes: Division 7 sets no age bar, because I was 14 or older`

@export I was a child when it happened. Can I be criminally responsible?
GIVEN `my age in whole years when it happened` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age, in completed years, when you did the conduct
      `the prosecution has proved I knew it was wrong` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the prosecution has proved that you knew your conduct was wrong (only asked if you were 10 to 13)
      `the question is still to be decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the question of what you knew has not yet been decided (TRUE before trial)
GIVETH A `Can I be responsible at my age`
`Can I be responsible at my age question` MEANS
    IF `my age in whole years when it happened` LESS THAN 10
    THEN `no: I was under 10, and a child under 10 is not criminally responsible for an offence (s 7.1)`
    ELSE IF `my age in whole years when it happened` LESS THAN 14
         THEN IF `the question is still to be decided`
              THEN `only if the prosecution proves I knew my conduct was wrong: I was 10 or more but under 14 (s 7.2)`
              ELSE IF `the prosecution has proved I knew it was wrong`
                   THEN `yes: I was 10 or more but under 14, and the prosecution has proved I knew my conduct was wrong (s 7.2)`
                   ELSE `no: I was 10 or more but under 14, and it is not proved that I knew my conduct was wrong (s 7.2)`
         ELSE `yes: Division 7 sets no age bar, because I was 14 or older`

-- s 7.3(1): "A person is not criminally responsible for an offence if, at the time of carrying out the conduct ..., the person
-- was suffering from a mental impairment that had the effect that: (a) the person did not know the nature and quality of the
-- conduct; or (b) the person did not know that the conduct was wrong ...; or (c) the person was unable to control the
-- conduct." (3): presumed not impaired unless "proved on the balance of probabilities (by the prosecution or the defence)".
-- (5): a "special verdict that a person is not guilty of an offence because of mental impairment". (8): "mental impairment
-- includes senility, intellectual disability, mental illness, brain damage and severe personality disorder"; (9) mental illness
-- excludes "the reaction of a healthy mind to extraordinary external stimuli" (asked as a fact).
DECLARE `Does my mental impairment excuse me` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I am not criminally responsible; the verdict must be the special verdict, not guilty because of mental impairment (s 7.3(1), (5))`
    `no: it is not proved on the balance of probabilities that I had a mental impairment with one of the s 7.3(1) effects, and I am presumed not to have (s 7.3(3))`

@export I had a mental illness or other impairment at the time. Am I criminally responsible?
GIVEN `it is proved I had a mental impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is proved, on the balance of probabilities, that you were suffering from a mental impairment (such as senility, intellectual disability, mental illness, brain damage or severe personality disorder) at the time of the conduct
      `because of it I did not know what I was doing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of it, you did not know the nature and quality of your conduct
      `because of it I did not know it was wrong` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of it, you could not reason with a moderate degree of sense and composure about whether your conduct, as reasonable people see it, was wrong
      `because of it I could not control it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of it, you were unable to control your conduct
GIVETH A `Does my mental impairment excuse me`
`Does my mental impairment excuse me question` MEANS
    IF `it is proved I had a mental impairment` AND (`because of it I did not know what I was doing` OR `because of it I did not know it was wrong` OR `because of it I could not control it`)
    THEN `yes: I am not criminally responsible; the verdict must be the special verdict, not guilty because of mental impairment (s 7.3(1), (5))`
    ELSE `no: it is not proved on the balance of probabilities that I had a mental impairment with one of the s 7.3(1) effects, and I am presumed not to have (s 7.3(3))`

§§ `Division 8 -- if you were intoxicated`

-- s 8.1: intoxication "is self-induced unless it came about: (a) involuntarily; or (b) as a result of fraud, sudden or
-- extraordinary emergency, accident, reasonable mistake, duress or force." s 8.5: "A person is not criminally responsible for
-- an offence if the person's conduct constituting the offence was as a result of intoxication that was not self-induced."
-- s 8.2(1): self-induced intoxication "cannot be considered in determining whether a fault element of basic intent existed";
-- (2) "basic intent is a fault element of intention for a physical element that consists only of conduct"; (3) it may be
-- considered on "whether conduct was accidental"; (4) and on a mistaken belief about facts "if the person had considered
-- whether or not the facts existed". s 8.3: for negligence, the standard of "a reasonable person who is not intoxicated" (or,
-- if not self-induced, intoxicated to the same extent). s 8.4: for a defence based on actual belief, intoxication "may be
-- considered" -- except (4) where every physical element has a fault element of basic intent; for a defence based on
-- reasonable belief, the standard of a sober reasonable person (or, if not self-induced, one intoxicated to the same extent).
-- s 4.2(6): not on voluntariness.
DECLARE `What my intoxication is asked about` IS ONE OF
    `whether my conduct was voluntary`
    `whether I intended my conduct (basic intent)`
    `whether I had another fault element: intention as to a circumstance or result, knowledge or recklessness`
    `whether I was negligent`
    `whether my conduct was an accident`
    `whether I had a belief a defence relies on`
    `whether my belief was reasonable, for a defence that needs a reasonable belief`

DECLARE `Can my intoxication help me` IS ONE OF
    `I am not criminally responsible at all: my conduct was the result of intoxication that was not self-induced (s 8.5)`
    `no: evidence of self-induced intoxication cannot be considered on this question (ss 4.2(6), 8.2(1))`
    `no: every physical element of this offence has a fault element of basic intent, so self-induced intoxication cannot be considered on the belief my defence relies on (s 8.4(4))`
    `yes, it may be considered (ss 8.2(3), (4), 8.4(1))`
    `yes, it may be considered, because I had considered whether the facts existed (s 8.2(4), (5))`
    `no, it does not help: the standard is that of a reasonable person who is not intoxicated (ss 8.3(1), 8.4(2))`
    `the standard is that of a reasonable person intoxicated to the same extent as I was, because my intoxication was not self-induced (ss 8.3(2), 8.4(3))`

@export I was drunk or high at the time. Can my intoxication be taken into account?
GIVEN `my intoxication was involuntary, or caused by fraud, emergency, accident, reasonable mistake, duress or force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your intoxication came about involuntarily, or because of fraud (for example a spiked drink), a sudden or extraordinary emergency, an accident, a reasonable mistake, duress or force; FALSE if you chose to drink or take the drug
      `my conduct was the result of that intoxication` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your conduct constituting the offence was a result of that intoxication
      `what it is asked about` IS A `What my intoxication is asked about` @desc What your intoxication is said to bear on
      `every element of the offence has a fault element of basic intent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether every physical element of the offence is conduct with intention as its fault element (no circumstance or result element with a fault element)
      `I had considered whether the facts existed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a mistaken belief about facts: whether you had considered whether the facts existed (or had on an earlier occasion, and honestly and reasonably believed this occasion was the same)
GIVETH A `Can my intoxication help me`
`Can my intoxication help me question` MEANS
    IF `my intoxication was involuntary, or caused by fraud, emergency, accident, reasonable mistake, duress or force` AND `my conduct was the result of that intoxication`
    THEN `I am not criminally responsible at all: my conduct was the result of intoxication that was not self-induced (s 8.5)`
    ELSE IF `my intoxication was involuntary, or caused by fraud, emergency, accident, reasonable mistake, duress or force`
         THEN CONSIDER `what it is asked about`
              WHEN `whether I was negligent` THEN `the standard is that of a reasonable person intoxicated to the same extent as I was, because my intoxication was not self-induced (ss 8.3(2), 8.4(3))`
              WHEN `whether my belief was reasonable, for a defence that needs a reasonable belief` THEN `the standard is that of a reasonable person intoxicated to the same extent as I was, because my intoxication was not self-induced (ss 8.3(2), 8.4(3))`
              OTHERWISE `yes, it may be considered (ss 8.2(3), (4), 8.4(1))`
         ELSE CONSIDER `what it is asked about`
              WHEN `whether my conduct was voluntary` THEN `no: evidence of self-induced intoxication cannot be considered on this question (ss 4.2(6), 8.2(1))`
              WHEN `whether I intended my conduct (basic intent)` THEN `no: evidence of self-induced intoxication cannot be considered on this question (ss 4.2(6), 8.2(1))`
              WHEN `whether I was negligent` THEN `no, it does not help: the standard is that of a reasonable person who is not intoxicated (ss 8.3(1), 8.4(2))`
              WHEN `whether my belief was reasonable, for a defence that needs a reasonable belief` THEN `no, it does not help: the standard is that of a reasonable person who is not intoxicated (ss 8.3(1), 8.4(2))`
              WHEN `whether I had a belief a defence relies on` THEN
                  IF `every element of the offence has a fault element of basic intent`
                  THEN `no: every physical element of this offence has a fault element of basic intent, so self-induced intoxication cannot be considered on the belief my defence relies on (s 8.4(4))`
                  ELSE `yes, it may be considered (ss 8.2(3), (4), 8.4(1))`
              WHEN `whether my conduct was an accident` THEN `yes, it may be considered (ss 8.2(3), (4), 8.4(1))`
              OTHERWISE `yes, it may be considered (ss 8.2(3), (4), 8.4(1))`

§§ `Division 9 -- if you made a mistake`

-- s 9.1(1): "not criminally responsible for an offence that has a physical element for which there is a fault element other
-- than negligence if: (a) at the time of the conduct ..., the person is under a mistaken belief about, or is ignorant of,
-- facts; and (b) the existence of that mistaken belief or ignorance negates any fault element applying to that physical
-- element." (2): the tribunal "may consider whether the mistaken belief or ignorance was reasonable" -- it need not be.
-- s 9.2(1): for a physical element with no fault element, not responsible if "(a) at or before the time of the conduct ..., the
-- person considered whether or not facts existed, and is under a mistaken but reasonable belief about those facts; and (b) had
-- those facts existed, the conduct would not have constituted an offence"; (2) an earlier occasion's consideration counts. s 6.2:
-- not for absolute liability. Negligence: neither s 9.1 nor s 9.2 (the negligence test itself absorbs the mistake).
DECLARE `How this element is made out` IS ONE OF
    `by a fault element other than negligence`
    `by negligence`
    `with strict liability`
    `with absolute liability`

DECLARE `Does my mistake of fact excuse me` IS ONE OF
    `yes: my mistaken belief or ignorance of the facts negates the fault element, so I am not criminally responsible; it need not have been reasonable (s 9.1)`
    `yes: I considered the facts and my mistaken belief was reasonable, and on the facts as I believed them there was no offence (s 9.2)`
    `no: my mistake or ignorance does not negate the fault element (s 9.1)`
    `no: for a strict liability element a mistake excuses only if I considered whether the facts existed, my belief was reasonable, and on those facts there would have been no offence (s 9.2)`
    `no: mistake of fact is no defence to an absolute liability element (s 6.2)`
    `no: Division 9 gives no mistake of fact defence for an element of negligence; whether I was negligent is decided by s 5.5`

@export I made a mistake about the facts. Does that excuse me?
GIVEN `how the element is made out` IS A `How this element is made out` @desc For the element your mistake is about: whether it needs a fault element (intention, knowledge or recklessness), or negligence, or is of strict liability, or of absolute liability
      `I was mistaken about, or ignorant of, the facts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the time of your conduct, you were under a mistaken belief about, or were ignorant of, the facts
      `my mistake negates the fault element` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, given your mistake or ignorance, you did not have the intention, knowledge or recklessness the element requires
      `I had considered whether the facts existed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether at or before the time, you considered whether the facts existed (or had on an earlier occasion, and honestly and reasonably believed this occasion was the same)
      `my belief was reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your mistaken belief was reasonable
      `on the facts I believed there was no offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, had the facts been as you believed, your conduct would not have been an offence
GIVETH A `Does my mistake of fact excuse me`
`Does my mistake of fact excuse me question` MEANS
    CONSIDER `how the element is made out`
    WHEN `by a fault element other than negligence` THEN
        IF `I was mistaken about, or ignorant of, the facts` AND `my mistake negates the fault element`
        THEN `yes: my mistaken belief or ignorance of the facts negates the fault element, so I am not criminally responsible; it need not have been reasonable (s 9.1)`
        ELSE `no: my mistake or ignorance does not negate the fault element (s 9.1)`
    WHEN `with strict liability` THEN
        IF `I was mistaken about, or ignorant of, the facts` AND `I had considered whether the facts existed` AND `my belief was reasonable` AND `on the facts I believed there was no offence`
        THEN `yes: I considered the facts and my mistaken belief was reasonable, and on the facts as I believed them there was no offence (s 9.2)`
        ELSE `no: for a strict liability element a mistake excuses only if I considered whether the facts existed, my belief was reasonable, and on those facts there would have been no offence (s 9.2)`
    WHEN `with absolute liability` THEN `no: mistake of fact is no defence to an absolute liability element (s 6.2)`
    OTHERWISE `no: Division 9 gives no mistake of fact defence for an element of negligence; whether I was negligent is decided by s 5.5`

-- s 9.3(1): "A person can be criminally responsible for an offence even if ... he or she is mistaken about, or ignorant of, the
-- existence or content of an Act that ... creates the offence or ... affects the scope or operation of the offence"; (2) unless
-- "the Act is expressly to the contrary effect". s 9.4(1): the same for subordinate legislation; (2) not if it is "expressly to
-- the contrary effect" or, at the time, it "(i) has not been made available to the public (by means of the Register under the
-- Legislation Act 2003 or otherwise); and (ii) has not otherwise been made available to persons likely to be affected by it in
-- such a way that the person would have become aware of its contents by exercising due diligence."
DECLARE `Kind of law I was mistaken about` IS ONE OF
    `an Act`
    `subordinate legislation (a regulation, rule or other legislative instrument)`

@export I did not know the law (or was mistaken about it). Does that excuse me?
GIVEN `the law I was mistaken about` IS A `Kind of law I was mistaken about` @desc Whether your mistake or ignorance was about an Act, or about subordinate legislation such as a regulation or legislative instrument
      `the law expressly says ignorance excuses` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act or instrument itself is expressly to the contrary effect (says that a person who does not know of it, or is mistaken about it, is not responsible)
      `the instrument was not available to the public or to me with due diligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For subordinate legislation: whether, at the time, it had not been made available to the public (on the Federal Register of Legislation or otherwise), and had not otherwise been made available to people likely to be affected so that you would have learned of it by exercising due diligence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my mistake of law excuse me` IF
        `the law expressly says ignorance excuses`
    OR  (`the law I was mistaken about` EQUALS `subordinate legislation (a regulation, rule or other legislative instrument)` AND `the instrument was not available to the public or to me with due diligence`)

-- s 9.5(1): "not criminally responsible for an offence that has a physical element relating to property if: (a) at the time of
-- the conduct ..., the person is under a mistaken belief about a proprietary or possessory right; and (b) the existence of that
-- right would negate a fault element for any physical element of the offence." (2): nor for "any other offence arising
-- necessarily out of the exercise of" that right. (3): "This section does not negate criminal responsibility for an offence
-- relating to the use of force against a person."
@export I thought the property was mine (or I was entitled to it). Does that excuse me?
GIVEN `the offence has an element relating to property, or arises necessarily from exercising the right` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence has a physical element relating to property, or arises necessarily out of exercising the right you believed you had
      `I mistakenly believed I had a right of ownership or possession` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the time, you were under a mistaken belief that you had a right of ownership or possession
      `that right would negate a fault element` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, had the right existed, you would not have had a fault element of the offence (for example you would not have been dishonest)
      `the offence is about using force against a person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence relates to the use of force against a person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my claim of right excuse me` IF
        `the offence has an element relating to property, or arises necessarily from exercising the right`
    AND `I mistakenly believed I had a right of ownership or possession`
    AND `that right would negate a fault element`
    AND (NOT `the offence is about using force against a person`)

§§ `Division 10 -- if something or someone else made you do it, or you were defending yourself`

-- s 10.1: "not criminally responsible for an offence that has a physical element to which absolute liability or strict
-- liability applies if: (a) the physical element is brought about by another person over whom the person has no control or by
-- a non-human act or event over which the person has no control; and (b) the person could not reasonably be expected to guard
-- against the bringing about of that physical element."
@export Something outside my control (another person, or an event) brought about part of the offence. Does that excuse me?
GIVEN `the element is of strict or absolute liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the element in question is one to which strict or absolute liability applies
      `it was brought about by someone or something I had no control over` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that element was brought about by another person over whom you had no control, or by a non-human act or event you had no control over
      `I could not reasonably have guarded against it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you could not reasonably be expected to have guarded against it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does intervening conduct or an event excuse me` IF
        `the element is of strict or absolute liability`
    AND `it was brought about by someone or something I had no control over`
    AND `I could not reasonably have guarded against it`

-- s 10.2(2): "A person carries out conduct under duress if and only if he or she reasonably believes that: (a) a threat has
-- been made that will be carried out unless an offence is committed; and (b) there is no reasonable way that the threat can be
-- rendered ineffective; and (c) the conduct is a reasonable response to the threat." (3): "This section does not apply if the
-- threat is made by or on behalf of a person with whom the person under duress is voluntarily associating for the purpose of
-- carrying out conduct of the kind actually carried out."
@export I was threatened into doing it. Does duress excuse me?
GIVEN `I reasonably believed a threat would be carried out unless I committed an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you reasonably believed a threat had been made that would be carried out unless an offence was committed
      `I reasonably believed there was no reasonable way to make the threat ineffective` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you reasonably believed there was no reasonable way to make the threat ineffective (for example by escaping or going to the police)
      `I reasonably believed my conduct was a reasonable response` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you reasonably believed your conduct was a reasonable response to the threat
      `the threat came from someone I was voluntarily associating with for this kind of conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the threat was made by or for a person you were voluntarily associating with for the purpose of doing conduct of this kind
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does duress excuse me` IF
        `I reasonably believed a threat would be carried out unless I committed an offence`
    AND `I reasonably believed there was no reasonable way to make the threat ineffective`
    AND `I reasonably believed my conduct was a reasonable response`
    AND (NOT `the threat came from someone I was voluntarily associating with for this kind of conduct`)

-- s 10.3(2): "applies if and only if the person carrying out the conduct reasonably believes that: (a) circumstances of sudden
-- or extraordinary emergency exist; and (b) committing the offence is the only reasonable way to deal with the emergency; and
-- (c) the conduct is a reasonable response to the emergency."
@export I did it to deal with a sudden emergency. Does that excuse me?
GIVEN `I reasonably believed there was a sudden or extraordinary emergency` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you reasonably believed circumstances of sudden or extraordinary emergency existed
      `I reasonably believed the offence was the only reasonable way to deal with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you reasonably believed committing the offence was the only reasonable way to deal with the emergency
      `I reasonably believed my conduct was a reasonable response to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you reasonably believed your conduct was a reasonable response to the emergency
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does sudden or extraordinary emergency excuse me` IF
        `I reasonably believed there was a sudden or extraordinary emergency`
    AND `I reasonably believed the offence was the only reasonable way to deal with it`
    AND `I reasonably believed my conduct was a reasonable response to it`

-- s 10.4(2): "A person carries out conduct in self-defence if and only if he or she believes the conduct is necessary: (a) to
-- defend himself or herself or another person; or (b) to prevent or terminate the unlawful imprisonment of himself or herself
-- or another person; or (c) to protect property from unlawful appropriation, destruction, damage or interference; or (d) to
-- prevent criminal trespass to any land or premises; or (e) to remove from any land or premises a person who is committing
-- criminal trespass; and the conduct is a reasonable response in the circumstances as he or she perceives them." (3): not
-- where "the person uses force that involves the intentional infliction of death or really serious injury" for (c), (d) or (e).
-- (4): not if "(a) the person is responding to lawful conduct; and (b) he or she knew that the conduct was lawful"; conduct "is
-- not lawful merely because the person carrying it out is not criminally responsible for it". The belief is my actual belief;
-- the reasonableness of the response is judged on the circumstances as I perceived them (asked as the court's finding).
DECLARE `What I was acting to do` IS ONE OF
    `defend myself or another person`
    `prevent or end the unlawful imprisonment of myself or another person`
    `protect property from unlawful taking, destruction, damage or interference`
    `prevent criminal trespass to land or premises`
    `remove someone committing criminal trespass from land or premises`

DECLARE `Does self-defence excuse me` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I believed my conduct was necessary for a purpose s 10.4(2) allows, and it was a reasonable response in the circumstances as I perceived them, so I am not criminally responsible (s 10.4(1), (2))`
    `no: I did not believe my conduct was necessary for that purpose, or it was not a reasonable response in the circumstances as I perceived them (s 10.4(2))`
    `no: I intentionally inflicted death or really serious injury to protect property or deal with a trespass, and self-defence never covers that (s 10.4(3))`
    `no: I was responding to conduct I knew was lawful (s 10.4(4))`

@export I was defending myself, someone else or my property. Does self-defence excuse me?
GIVEN `what I was acting to do` IS A `What I was acting to do` @desc What you believed your conduct was necessary for
      `I believed my conduct was necessary for that` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you actually believed your conduct was necessary for that purpose
      `the court finds it a reasonable response as I perceived things` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court (or jury) finds your conduct was a reasonable response in the circumstances as you perceived them
      `I intentionally inflicted death or really serious injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you used force involving the intentional infliction of death or really serious injury
      `I was responding to conduct I knew was lawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct you were responding to was lawful (for example a lawful arrest) and you knew it was lawful
GIVETH A `Does self-defence excuse me`
`Does self-defence excuse me question` MEANS
    IF `I was responding to conduct I knew was lawful`
    THEN `no: I was responding to conduct I knew was lawful (s 10.4(4))`
    ELSE IF `I intentionally inflicted death or really serious injury` AND (NOT (`what I was acting to do` EQUALS `defend myself or another person`)) AND (NOT (`what I was acting to do` EQUALS `prevent or end the unlawful imprisonment of myself or another person`))
         THEN `no: I intentionally inflicted death or really serious injury to protect property or deal with a trespass, and self-defence never covers that (s 10.4(3))`
         ELSE IF `I believed my conduct was necessary for that` AND `the court finds it a reasonable response as I perceived things`
              THEN `yes: I believed my conduct was necessary for a purpose s 10.4(2) allows, and it was a reasonable response in the circumstances as I perceived them, so I am not criminally responsible (s 10.4(1), (2))`
              ELSE `no: I did not believe my conduct was necessary for that purpose, or it was not a reasonable response in the circumstances as I perceived them (s 10.4(2))`

-- s 10.5: "A person is not criminally responsible for an offence if the conduct constituting the offence is justified or excused
-- by or under a law."
@export A law allowed me to do what I did. Does that excuse me?
GIVEN `a law justified or excused my conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your conduct was justified or excused by or under a law (for example a licence, permit or statutory authority)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does lawful authority excuse me` IF
    `a law justified or excused my conduct`

§§ `Part 2.6 -- who has to prove what`

-- s 13.1(1): "The prosecution bears a legal burden of proving every element of an offence relevant to the guilt of the person
-- charged." (2): and "of disproving any matter in relation to which the defendant has discharged an evidential burden". s 13.2:
-- "beyond reasonable doubt" unless the law creating the offence says otherwise. s 13.3(1): "a burden of proof that a law imposes
-- on a defendant is an evidential burden only"; (2) a defendant relying on Part 2.3 "(other than section 7.3) bears an
-- evidential burden"; (3) the same for "any exception, exemption, excuse, qualification or justification provided by the law
-- creating an offence"; (6) evidential burden: "adducing or pointing to evidence that suggests a reasonable possibility that the
-- matter exists or does not exist"; (4) discharged also by the prosecution's or the court's evidence. s 13.4: a legal burden on
-- the defendant "if and only if the law expressly: (a) specifies that the burden ... is a legal burden; or (b) requires the
-- defendant to prove the matter; or (c) creates a presumption that the matter exists unless the contrary is proved." s 13.5:
-- "on the balance of probabilities". s 7.3(3): mental impairment is proved on the balance of probabilities by whichever side
-- relies on it; s 7.2(2): a child's knowledge of wrongness is for the prosecution.
DECLARE `The matter to be proved` IS ONE OF
    `an element of the offence`
    `a defence in Part 2.3 (other than mental impairment), such as duress or self-defence`
    `an exception, exemption, excuse, qualification or justification in the law creating the offence`
    `a matter the law expressly requires me to prove, calls a legal burden, or presumes unless I prove otherwise`
    `mental impairment`
    `that I knew my conduct was wrong, when I was 10 to 13`

DECLARE `Who must prove it` IS ONE OF
    `the prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt (ss 13.1(1), 13.2(1))`
    `the prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt: I have pointed to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility of it, so the prosecution must disprove it (ss 13.1(2), 13.2, 13.3)`
    `I bear only an evidential burden: I must point to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility of it (from my own evidence, the prosecution's or the court's); then the prosecution must disprove it beyond reasonable doubt (s 13.3)`
    `I bear a legal burden, on the balance of probabilities (ss 13.4, 13.5)`
    `whoever relies on it, on the balance of probabilities; I am presumed not to have had one (s 7.3(3))`

@export Who has to prove this matter at my trial, and to what standard?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `The matter to be proved` @desc The matter you are asking about
      `evidence of a reasonable possibility of it has been pointed to` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a defence, exception or excuse: whether you (or the prosecution's evidence, or the court) have adduced or pointed to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility that it exists
GIVETH A `Who must prove it`
`Who must prove it question` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the matter`
    WHEN `an element of the offence` THEN `the prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt (ss 13.1(1), 13.2(1))`
    WHEN `that I knew my conduct was wrong, when I was 10 to 13` THEN `the prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt (ss 13.1(1), 13.2(1))`
    WHEN `a matter the law expressly requires me to prove, calls a legal burden, or presumes unless I prove otherwise` THEN `I bear a legal burden, on the balance of probabilities (ss 13.4, 13.5)`
    WHEN `mental impairment` THEN `whoever relies on it, on the balance of probabilities; I am presumed not to have had one (s 7.3(3))`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `evidence of a reasonable possibility of it has been pointed to`
        THEN `the prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt: I have pointed to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility of it, so the prosecution must disprove it (ss 13.1(2), 13.2, 13.3)`
        ELSE `I bear only an evidential burden: I must point to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility of it (from my own evidence, the prosecution's or the court's); then the prosecution must disprove it beyond reasonable doubt (s 13.3)`

§§ `s 474.17 -- if you are accused of menacing, harassing or offensive phone or online use`

-- s 474.17(1): "A person commits an offence if: (a) the person uses a carriage service; and (b) the person does so in a way
-- (whether by the method of use or the content of a communication, or both) that reasonable persons would regard as being, in
-- all the circumstances, menacing, harassing or offensive. Penalty: Imprisonment for 5 years." No fault element is stated, so
-- by s 5.6: intention for (a), the conduct of using a carriage service; recklessness for (b), which this encoding reads as a
-- circumstance (NOTES.md finding 10 and fork F1, an interpretive choice; the other reading is below). Whether reasonable persons would so regard it is for the tribunal
-- of fact, taking into account for offensiveness "(a) the standards of morality, decency and propriety generally accepted by
-- reasonable adults; (b) the literary, artistic or educational merit ...; (c) the general character of the material" (s 473.4).
-- "Carriage service" (a phone, internet, messaging or social media service) is asked as a fact. A Part 2.3 defence is asked as
-- one fact; the questions above answer each.
DECLARE `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence` IS ONE OF
    `yes, if proved: every element and fault element is made out and no defence applies; the maximum penalty is imprisonment for 5 years (s 474.17(1))`
    `no: I did not use a carriage service, or did not mean to (ss 474.17(1)(a), 5.6(1))`
    `no: the court does not find that reasonable persons would regard my use, in all the circumstances, as menacing, harassing or offensive (ss 474.17(1)(b), 473.4)`
    `no: I was not reckless (nor knew or intended) that reasonable persons would regard my use as menacing, harassing or offensive (ss 5.6(2), 5.4)`
    `no: a defence in Part 2.3 applies, so I am not criminally responsible`
    `no: I did not mean to use the carriage service in the way I did (by that method or with that content); on the conduct reading that is the only fault element for paragraph (1)(b) (ss 474.17(1)(b), 5.6(1); fork F1)`

-- FORK F1 (NOTES.md "Forks"; ledger au:REQ-0103). Section 474.17 states no fault element. Which s 5.6 default reaches paragraph
-- (1)(b) depends on what kind of physical element it is (s 4.1(1): "(a) conduct; or (b) a result of conduct; or (c) a
-- circumstance in which conduct, or a result of conduct, occurs"), and no deposited decision settles it:
--   (a) a circumstance -- that the way of use is one reasonable persons would regard as menacing, harassing or offensive --
--       so recklessness is the fault element (s 5.6(2)): I must have been aware of a substantial risk that reasonable persons
--       would so regard it, and taking the risk was unjustifiable (s 5.4(1), (3)). This encoding's default.
--   (b) part of the conduct -- "uses a carriage service ... in a way ..." -- so intention is the fault element (s 5.6(1)): I
--       must have meant to use the service in the way I did (meant to send what I sent, by that method), and whether reasonable
--       persons would regard that way as menacing, harassing or offensive is for the tribunal of fact alone, with no awareness
--       of it required of me.
-- The reading is an input, so each can be asserted; the original question below takes reading (a).
DECLARE `Reading of s 474.17(1)(b)` IS ONE OF
    `(a) a circumstance, carrying recklessness (s 5.6(2)) -- the default reading`
    `(b) part of the conduct, carrying intention (s 5.6(1))`

DECLARE `What must be proved about my state of mind under s 474.17` IS ONE OF
    `that I meant to use the carriage service, and that I was aware of a substantial risk that reasonable persons would regard my use as menacing, harassing or offensive and, knowing the circumstances, it was unjustifiable to take that risk (or that I knew or intended it) (ss 5.6(1), (2), 5.4)`
    `only that I meant to use the carriage service in the way I did (meant to send what I sent, by that method); whether reasonable persons would regard it as menacing, harassing or offensive is for the court or jury, with no awareness of it required of me (ss 5.6(1), 4.1)`

@export I am charged under s 474.17. What must the prosecution prove about my state of mind: that I was aware my messages risked being seen as menacing, harassing or offensive, or only that I meant to send them?
GIVEN `the reading` IS A `Reading of s 474.17(1)(b)` @desc Which reading of s 474.17(1)(b) to apply: (a) it is a circumstance, so recklessness applies (the encoding's default); or (b) it is part of the conduct, so only intention to use the service that way is needed. No deposited court decision settles which is right
GIVETH A `What must be proved about my state of mind under s 474.17`
`What must be proved about my state of mind under s 474.17 question` MEANS
    IF `the reading` EQUALS `(b) part of the conduct, carrying intention (s 5.6(1))`
    THEN `only that I meant to use the carriage service in the way I did (meant to send what I sent, by that method); whether reasonable persons would regard it as menacing, harassing or offensive is for the court or jury, with no awareness of it required of me (ss 5.6(1), 4.1)`
    ELSE `that I meant to use the carriage service, and that I was aware of a substantial risk that reasonable persons would regard my use as menacing, harassing or offensive and, knowing the circumstances, it was unjustifiable to take that risk (or that I knew or intended it) (ss 5.6(1), (2), 5.4)`

@export I sent messages or posted online. On a chosen reading of s 474.17(1)(b), have I committed the offence of using a carriage service to menace, harass or cause offence?
GIVEN `the reading` IS A `Reading of s 474.17(1)(b)` @desc Which reading of s 474.17(1)(b) to apply: (a) a circumstance, carrying recklessness (the default); or (b) part of the conduct, carrying intention. No deposited court decision settles which is right
      `I meant to use a phone, internet or messaging service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you used a carriage service (a phone, the internet, a messaging app or social media) and meant to do so
      `I meant to use it in the way I did` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you meant to use it in the way you did: meant to send or post what you sent or posted, by the method you used (read only on reading (b))
      `the court finds reasonable persons would regard it as menacing, harassing or offensive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court (or jury) finds that reasonable persons would regard the way you used it (how you did it, what you sent, or both) as menacing, harassing or offensive in all the circumstances
      `I was aware of a substantial risk that it would be so regarded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc On reading (a): whether you were aware of a substantial risk that reasonable persons would regard it that way (or knew or believed they would)
      `the court finds taking that risk was unjustifiable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc On reading (a): whether the court finds that, given the circumstances known to you, taking that risk was unjustifiable
      `a Part 2.3 defence applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a defence in Part 2.3 of the Code applies (for example duress, self-defence, involuntary intoxication, lawful authority)
GIVETH A `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence`
`Have I committed the s 474.17 offence on a reading question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I meant to use a phone, internet or messaging service`)
    THEN `no: I did not use a carriage service, or did not mean to (ss 474.17(1)(a), 5.6(1))`
    ELSE IF (NOT `the court finds reasonable persons would regard it as menacing, harassing or offensive`)
         THEN `no: the court does not find that reasonable persons would regard my use, in all the circumstances, as menacing, harassing or offensive (ss 474.17(1)(b), 473.4)`
         ELSE IF `the reading` EQUALS `(b) part of the conduct, carrying intention (s 5.6(1))` AND (NOT `I meant to use it in the way I did`)
              THEN `no: I did not mean to use the carriage service in the way I did (by that method or with that content); on the conduct reading that is the only fault element for paragraph (1)(b) (ss 474.17(1)(b), 5.6(1); fork F1)`
              ELSE IF `the reading` EQUALS `(a) a circumstance, carrying recklessness (s 5.6(2)) -- the default reading` AND (NOT (`I was aware of a substantial risk that it would be so regarded` AND `the court finds taking that risk was unjustifiable`))
                   THEN `no: I was not reckless (nor knew or intended) that reasonable persons would regard my use as menacing, harassing or offensive (ss 5.6(2), 5.4)`
                   ELSE IF `a Part 2.3 defence applies`
                        THEN `no: a defence in Part 2.3 applies, so I am not criminally responsible`
                        ELSE `yes, if proved: every element and fault element is made out and no defence applies; the maximum penalty is imprisonment for 5 years (s 474.17(1))`

@export I sent messages or posted online. Have I committed the offence of using a carriage service to menace, harass or cause offence?
GIVEN `I meant to use a phone, internet or messaging service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you used a carriage service (a phone, the internet, a messaging app or social media) and meant to do so
      `the court finds reasonable persons would regard it as menacing, harassing or offensive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court (or jury) finds that reasonable persons would regard the way you used it (how you did it, what you sent, or both) as menacing, harassing or offensive in all the circumstances
      `I was aware of a substantial risk that it would be so regarded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were aware of a substantial risk that reasonable persons would regard it that way (or knew or believed they would)
      `the court finds taking that risk was unjustifiable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that, given the circumstances known to you, taking that risk was unjustifiable
      `a Part 2.3 defence applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a defence in Part 2.3 of the Code applies (for example duress, self-defence, involuntary intoxication, lawful authority)
GIVETH A `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence`
-- The default reading (fork F1 (a)). `I meant to use it in the way I did` is not read on reading (a); TRUE is a placeholder.
`Have I committed the s 474.17 offence question` MEANS
    `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence on a reading question` `(a) a circumstance, carrying recklessness (s 5.6(2)) -- the default reading` `I meant to use a phone, internet or messaging service` TRUE `the court finds reasonable persons would regard it as menacing, harassing or offensive` `I was aware of a substantial risk that it would be so regarded` `the court finds taking that risk was unjustifiable` `a Part 2.3 defence applies`

§§ `s 135.1 -- if you are accused of dishonesty against a Commonwealth entity (such as Centrelink or the ATO)`

-- s 135.1(1): "A person commits an offence if: (a) the person does anything with the intention of dishonestly obtaining a gain
-- from another person; and (b) the other person is a Commonwealth entity. Penalty: Imprisonment for 10 years." (2): "it is not
-- necessary to prove that the defendant knew that the other person was a Commonwealth entity." (3), (4): the same for
-- "dishonestly causing a loss". (5): "(a) the person dishonestly causes a loss, or dishonestly causes a risk of loss, to another
-- person; and (b) the first-mentioned person knows or believes that the loss will occur or that there is a substantial risk of
-- the loss occurring; and (c) the other person is a Commonwealth entity"; (6): "Absolute liability applies to the paragraph
-- (5)(c) element". s 130.3: "dishonest means: (a) dishonest according to the standards of ordinary people; and (b) known by
-- the defendant to be dishonest according to the standards of ordinary people." Whether conduct is dishonest by ordinary
-- standards is the tribunal of fact's; what I knew of those standards is a fact about me. "Commonwealth entity" (s 130.1, the
-- Commonwealth or a Commonwealth authority) is asked as a fact.
DECLARE `Kind of s 135.1 charge` IS ONE OF
    `obtaining a gain (s 135.1(1))`
    `causing a loss (s 135.1(3))`
    `causing a loss or risk of loss I knew or believed would happen (s 135.1(5))`

DECLARE `Have I committed general dishonesty` IS ONE OF
    `yes, if proved: every element is made out and no defence applies; the maximum penalty is imprisonment for 10 years (s 135.1)`
    `no: the other person is not a Commonwealth entity (s 135.1(1)(b), (3)(b), (5)(c))`
    `no: what I did was not dishonest by the standards of ordinary people, or I did not know it was (s 130.3)`
    `no: I did not act with the intention of obtaining a gain from, or causing a loss to, the Commonwealth entity (s 135.1(1)(a), (3)(a))`
    `no: I did not cause a loss or risk of loss, or did not know or believe that it would occur or that there was a substantial risk of it (s 135.1(5)(a), (b))`
    `no: a defence in Part 2.3 applies, so I am not criminally responsible`

@export I am accused of dishonestly getting money from, or causing a loss to, a Commonwealth agency. Have I committed general dishonesty (s 135.1)?
GIVEN `the charge` IS A `Kind of s 135.1 charge` @desc Which form of general dishonesty you are charged with
      `the other person is a Commonwealth entity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you are said to have obtained from or caused loss to is the Commonwealth or a Commonwealth authority (for example Services Australia (Centrelink, Medicare) or the ATO); it need not be proved you knew this
      `the court finds it dishonest by ordinary standards` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court (or jury) finds what you did was dishonest according to the standards of ordinary people
      `I knew it was dishonest by ordinary standards` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you knew it was dishonest according to the standards of ordinary people
      `I acted intending to obtain a gain or cause a loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a gain or loss charge: whether you did something meaning to obtain a gain (money or other property, kept or obtained) from the entity, or to cause it a loss
      `I caused a loss or risk of loss and knew or believed it would happen` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a s 135.1(5) charge: whether you caused the entity a loss or a risk of loss and knew or believed the loss would occur, or that there was a substantial risk of it
      `a Part 2.3 defence applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a defence in Part 2.3 of the Code applies (for example duress, a mistake of fact negating dishonesty, a claim of right)
GIVETH A `Have I committed general dishonesty`
`Have I committed general dishonesty question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the other person is a Commonwealth entity`)
    THEN `no: the other person is not a Commonwealth entity (s 135.1(1)(b), (3)(b), (5)(c))`
    ELSE IF (NOT (`the court finds it dishonest by ordinary standards` AND `I knew it was dishonest by ordinary standards`))
         THEN `no: what I did was not dishonest by the standards of ordinary people, or I did not know it was (s 130.3)`
         ELSE IF (`the charge` EQUALS `causing a loss or risk of loss I knew or believed would happen (s 135.1(5))`) AND (NOT `I caused a loss or risk of loss and knew or believed it would happen`)
              THEN `no: I did not cause a loss or risk of loss, or did not know or believe that it would occur or that there was a substantial risk of it (s 135.1(5)(a), (b))`
              ELSE IF (NOT (`the charge` EQUALS `causing a loss or risk of loss I knew or believed would happen (s 135.1(5))`)) AND (NOT `I acted intending to obtain a gain or cause a loss`)
                   THEN `no: I did not act with the intention of obtaining a gain from, or causing a loss to, the Commonwealth entity (s 135.1(1)(a), (3)(a))`
                   ELSE IF `a Part 2.3 defence applies`
                        THEN `no: a defence in Part 2.3 applies, so I am not criminally responsible`
                        ELSE `yes, if proved: every element is made out and no defence applies; the maximum penalty is imprisonment for 10 years (s 135.1)`
